name issue_24_timing_v2_high_jitter
describe 40 ms jitter with reordering, every v2 switch and libwebrtc's receive timing (timing v2): video delay at libwebrtc's level (issue #24)
duration 30s
at 0s capacity=3000kbps delay=30ms jitter=40ms reorder=on queue=300ms
cc v2
pacer v2
tcc v2
rtx v2
rtcp v2
keyframe v2
allocator v2
packetization v2
audio v2
timing v2
# libwebrtc's JitterEstimator (2.33 sigma - 30 ms noise threshold plus 10 ms
# OS jitter, jitter_estimator.cc) and VCMTiming's current delay, which only
# grows when a frame is released late (timing.cc UpdateCurrentDelay).
# Seeds 1-10, video.e2e_delay_p95_ms: timing v1 162-313 (mean 202); timing v2
# 130-199 (mean 152); libwebrtc on high_jitter 122-152 (mean 135, parity-data
# main be4bfbc). video.e2e_delay_p50_ms: timing v1 158-245 (mean 175); timing
# v2 95-157 (mean 131); libwebrtc 92. Reordered packets get NACKed, so the
# estimator adds 0.9 x RTT once 3 frames were NACKed; the bench's receiver
# knows the sender's RTT, while libwebrtc's receive-only Call in sm_compare
# has none (without the RTT term ours is p50 83-98, p95 130-199).
expect video.e2e_delay_p95_ms < 250
expect video.e2e_delay_p50_ms < 170
expect video.freeze_total_ms < 500
expect video.render_fps >= 29
